[Music]  welcome back to cbs mornings at 7 30. a  cbs news investigation into allegations  of fraud waste and abuse at the  department of alcohol tobacco firearms  and explosives is now prompting  congressional action hundreds of  millions of taxpayer dollars may be  involved here senior investigative  correspondent catherine harris broke the  story and she's live for us now at the  agency's headquarters catherine good  morning to you  good morning the top republican on the  senate committee with oversight says  he's got deep concerns that the atf may  have broken the rules when it gave bonus  pay to employees at taxpayers expense  after an atf whistleblower who asked to  be disguised and not to use his last  name when on the record with cbs news  how big is the bump up in pay 25  as soon as joe an army veteran got to  the atf's human resources department in  2016 he said he noticed personnel  problems they would bring every  agent that wanted to be promoted to the  headquarters that would spend time there  doing a whole lot of jobs desk jobs desk  jobs a variety of jobs to which they  weren't even minimally qualified and joe  told us there was another issue even  though some atf personnel were doing  administrative jobs joe told us they  were paid a bonus known as law  enforcement availability pay or leap for  short people were receiving it in  positions where they weren't authorized  government regulations stipulate the  extra pay is reserved for criminal  investigators who are on call and  expected to work on scheduled additional  hours how much money are we talking  about for an individual employee if you  were making a hundred thousand dollars  and you got leave you would get 125 000.  joe doesn't know how long it's been  going on or how much money has been  wasted but colleagues told them that was  the atf way what do you mean the atf way  well this is what they've done for 30  plus years  to what i was told after telling a  senior atf official in 2018 what he'd  found joe got this email from his  supervisor saying his bosses were upset  joe's performance reviews went from  fully successful before going to  management to minimally successful after  what did you  take away is the message don't look over  there just because you see someone  stealing money at the atm you don't have  to say anything in june last year a  federal lawyer told joe the office of  special counsel found a substantial  likelihood of wrongdoing since the  government's hr office suspended the  atf's authority to create certain jobs  for no less than six months this seems  to be a waste of taxpayers money senator  chuck grassley is the top republican on  the senate committee with direct  oversight of the atf while four agencies  are investigating joe's case over a  three-year period none of it reached  grassley's office should someone have  told your committee you're absolutely  right it boils down to the fact that  agencies don't like to be  embarrassed by these revelations that  come from whistleblowers because of the  cbs report grassley's investigators sent  these letters to the atf the justice  department and the federal government's  hr agency demanding records and answers  will you ask your investigators to find  out if it's happening at other agencies  well my suspicion is it probably is  we're going to find out how widespread  it is four months ago joe lost his job  his supervisor said he was inconsistent  following instructions and argumentative  among other issues are you a difficult  employee if what you're asking me to do  is illegal unethical immoral yes i'm  going to be difficult because i'm not  going to do it  an email from the office of special  counsel reviewed by cbs news says a  final report into joe's claims is  delayed citing broad implications for  the atf four government agencies  declined to answer our questions and now  they'll have to respond to the senator  later this month nate  catherine thank you

